      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $a_{33}=-2$ is in the same column as the leading entry $a_{23}=6$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -9 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the first row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 7 & -3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $c_{22}=1$ is to the left of the leading entry $c_{13}=5$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form.",
